WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:05.130
I just want to start off this video
by saying I am deeply, deeply sorry.

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:09.634
I've been deceiving you guys
for the last two years.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.555
You all think I live in this nice place

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:16.433
with this nice brick fireplace,

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:20.437
but this room is actually

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:23.231
a total mess.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.819
So when we moved in here,
we had so many plans for this room,

00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:31.698
we were going to use it for hanging out,
for watching TV, for reading,

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:34.659
but then I started my YouTube channel

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:38.413
and it just kind of became a filming room

00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:42.667
and I just totally neglected it
pretty badly.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:45.837
And it's been being used for just storage
pretty much.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:50.050
So now we're finally taking the step
to make this place a little bit nicer.

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:55.680
And so today I want to show you my thought
process for transforming this place

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.102
into a more livable and functional space
by just using my iPad.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.272
A portion of this video is sponsored
by Wayfair.

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.901
More on their awesome
YouTube series later in this video.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.819
As I've mentioned
in some of my other videos,

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:13.490
my design workflow has changed completely
since I got my iPad.

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:17.619
I use a combination of procreate
and more folio for most of my design work,

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:21.039
and if you want you can check out
some of the brushes and backgrounds

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:24.417
that I use on a daily basis
in the link, in the description.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.545
So I have gone ahead
and cleaned up the room

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:30.548
a little bit
just so that I can show you around.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.593
And so I do have a fairly

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:36.179
interesting
set of constraints in this room.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.265
So the filming room is in the basement.

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:42.310
So first of all, it's got a pretty low
ceiling height.

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.188
It's about eight feet, which is about

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:48.608
2.4 meters for the rest of the world.

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:52.529
And I have this very narrow

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:55.323
strip window and very little daylight,

00:01:55.698 --> 00:02:00.620
which is actually perfect
for filming over here.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:06.459
I have my filming setup,
which is constantly, constantly a mess.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.754
I've got my camera and my

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:14.175
equipment, my lenses, my cables
all on this shelf,

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:17.512
and I have my camera right in the center.

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:20.807
And sometimes Raf will sit here
and hold me.

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:24.602
And typically, I'll have, like,
a bunch of lights around here,

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:30.191
and I'm like, sitting here with my laptop
and my notes

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:34.070
and I do like to keep the set up
fairly permanent

00:02:34.612 --> 00:02:38.283
because it just removes
a lot of the friction out of filming.

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:42.579
And if any of you guys are interested
in starting YouTube channels,

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:45.874
I feel like that's
one of the easiest things

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:49.043
that you can do
to keep your videos consistent.

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.588
Just to have like a permanent setup.

00:02:52.046 --> 00:02:58.261
And over here, I have the couch right now

00:02:58.303 --> 00:03:02.223
and I have this really ugly bulkhead
sticking down

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:07.395
and it brings down the ceiling
to about six foot eight.

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:10.064
I think that's about two meters.

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:13.568
And it is in the same datum

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:16.112
as the top of this window.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.908
So because this room feels very like

00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:23.203
fragmented right now,
I'm going to try to use this datum

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:27.081
to try to create
a little bit of order in the space.

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:30.126
And, of course, over here

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:32.962
I have my filming backdrop,

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:36.966
and I am kind of considering
whitewashing this.

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:39.344
I don't know.

00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:41.638
I'm getting a little bit
sick of this red fireplace.

00:03:42.347 --> 00:03:44.390
Are you guys getting a little bit
sick of it?

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.642
I'm getting a little bit sick of it.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.560
So we'll see.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.481
But even when I'm not filming,
it is kind of the hearth of this room.

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:54.567
It's the place
where your eyes are naturally drawn

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.944
to when you enter the room.

00:03:55.944 --> 00:04:01.366
And because the space is quite small,
with a fairly low ceiling height,

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:05.036
I want to visually extend
the length of this room a little bit.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.121
And when I first moved in here,

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:11.542
that was the intent of these low
horizontal shelves.

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:15.546
We just cut up some wooden panels
and set them on these concrete blocks.

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.841
But over time,
we realized that they're great

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:22.053
for displaying things,
but they're not very functional

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:24.973
and the spaces got really messy,
really fast.

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:30.019
So before I had my iPad,
I would have probably drawn up

00:04:30.019 --> 00:04:33.356
an actual true elevation
and started sketching on that.

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:35.441
But these days I find it

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:39.570
the easiest to just like take a photo
and start sketching on top of that.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:44.575
This way I can really just
quickly sketch out some ideas and see

00:04:44.575 --> 00:04:48.746
which ones I want to develop further,
whether it's in a 3D model or in a render.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.792
And it's a really fast way
for me to try out different options.

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:57.005
And as I'm doing this,
I am simultaneously going on Pinterest

00:04:57.005 --> 00:05:00.758
and picking out some inspiration
images for the room, because that's,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:04.971
I think, one of the most fun parts
of an interior design project.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:08.766
I think my style preference is somewhere
between mid-century

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:12.979
modern and Scandinavian,
but I also love the rough natural textures

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:17.233
of that Mediterranean
or the Japanese wabi sabi look.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:22.447
But as I was finding these images,
I kept seeing the term Japan,

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:27.660
the independiente of Handy, and I discover
that there is a very specific style.

00:05:27.702 --> 00:05:30.663
That's the perfect combination
of all of these.

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:34.167
And actually, I learned this
through Wayfair's YouTube channel.

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:38.171
I knew Wayfair as a website
where you can buy affordable, chic

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:42.967
looking furniture, but I never knew that
they had an educational YouTube channel.

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:47.221
They have a new series called
A Style is Born, where they discuss

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:50.350
how and why certain styles came to be.

00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:54.979
I love how they break down the history
of that style from the social

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:58.900
and economic conditions
and why it's making a comeback.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.987
And I think when you understand
the context behind something,

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:07.033
you can appreciate it a lot more beyond
just the esthetics of it.

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:11.287
It can also help you understand
why you're drawn to that specific chair

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:12.997
or table or vase.

00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:16.376
Anyways, I would highly recommend
you check out their episode on Japan.

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:21.214
It's really well produce, it's really well
researched and it's really fun to watch.

00:06:21.255 --> 00:06:23.633
So yeah, go subscribe to their channel.

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:26.052
It's full of really great content.

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.096
All include the link in the description.

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:32.225
So I'm going to put this photo
on as my bass player and the first thing

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:36.479
that I do is I'm going to set up the grids
and I'm going to use this

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:40.316
to set up my baselines
and then I adjust the photo

00:06:40.316 --> 00:06:44.195
to have straight horizontals and verticals
as much as possible

00:06:44.195 --> 00:06:54.414
without going into Photoshop
and fixing up the photo.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:58.626
And then I'll put on the perspective grid
and I'm just going to leave this

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:02.296
on throughout the duration
of this process, try to align

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:05.508
the perspective lines as best as you can

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:08.469
and create another layer on top.

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:14.559
So this layer is going to be your base,
outline your walls and your doors

00:07:14.559 --> 00:07:19.564
or your windows and basically elements
that are already in the space.

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:23.234
Once you do this, you're going to have
some lines that naturally come out,

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:27.280
and I'm using that as my starting point
for the design.

00:07:27.363 --> 00:07:29.407
We've got the strip window

00:07:29.407 --> 00:07:33.286
and this bulkhead that are kind of more
or less on the same level.

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:38.249
And we also have this wall
that drags in a couple of inches.

00:07:38.249 --> 00:07:43.171
And because this room
has a lot of fragmented elements,

00:07:43.671 --> 00:07:46.340
I want to use this as a datum
to try to bring

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.594
some order into the space
before I start sketching.

00:07:49.927 --> 00:07:54.265
I draw out a very rough
plan of the options I want to look at.

00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:56.392
I'm going to need about eight boxes.

00:07:56.517 --> 00:08:00.521
Those are Kallax boxes of storage,
which probably means

00:08:00.521 --> 00:08:05.818
I should get about 12 to 16
because, you know, I want to be realistic.

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:10.156
I think a good rule of thumb
for life is to account

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:13.451
for double the amount of storage
that you think you're going to need.

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:17.580
So I want to explore an option
where I have a large storage unit

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.335
that goes up to this wall
jog and an option where I have low storage

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:28.216
units broken up into two sections and
maybe I'll do some iterations off of that.

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:31.177
I'll have my desk
and my camera set up right here,

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:35.598
and sometimes my partner sits right
next to the camera to help me film.

00:08:35.598 --> 00:08:40.353
And so if you have these non-negotiable
constraints like this,

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:44.815
you can just put them
right into the base drawing.

00:08:45.441 --> 00:08:49.237
So this large storage unit,
I'd align it for our datum,

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:53.324
and maybe this corner
can be its own thing.

00:08:53.491 --> 00:08:58.246
So what I'm doing here with the curtains
is I'm pulling it just past my window

00:08:58.246 --> 00:09:00.206
all the way to the end of the wall.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:03.668
So it looks like the window continues
all the way through.

00:09:03.668 --> 00:09:07.838
And I'm starting the curtain right
from the ceiling all the way to the floor.

00:09:08.256 --> 00:09:10.383
So it adds some height to the space.

00:09:10.383 --> 00:09:16.138
But as you can see already,
I think the storage unit is a little bit

00:09:16.138 --> 00:09:19.600
big for the space,
especially because we've got this very low

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.771
ceiling height and it's already starting
to feel a little bit overwhelming.

00:09:24.021 --> 00:09:27.191
So we can try something
a little bit smaller,

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:31.779
but that just doesn't really work
with any of the grids.

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:34.907
So it just looks a little bit
awkward in the space.

00:09:35.032 --> 00:09:39.370
I think a lower storage works
a lot better with the proportions

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:44.667
of this particular space, maybe
with a wall shelf along the state m line.

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:48.671
And you can see how I've done something
really similar with my home office,

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:51.549
which is also really fragmented.

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:55.386
Like there's a window
that's off the center, there's

00:09:55.386 --> 00:09:59.348
this bulkhead and there's doors
in kind of awkward places.

00:09:59.348 --> 00:10:04.437
But in this case, I was able to create
a continuous perimeter with the shelving,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.857
so I decided to put lights to up

00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:10.901
like the ceiling
and give the illusion of a taller space.

00:10:10.901 --> 00:10:14.322
But with this room, I'm
not going to be able to continue this line

00:10:14.322 --> 00:10:17.325
across the room,
so I'm not going to be updating this.

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:20.661
And instead I'm probably

00:10:20.661 --> 00:10:23.956
going to use it for storing books
or my models

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:27.335
or anything that I don't reach
on a very regular basis.

00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:31.839
So proportionally, I think these smaller
shelves work a lot better.

00:10:32.089 --> 00:10:33.466
So I like how this option's

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:38.012
creating its own little zone,
and I'll probably put it in like an area

00:10:38.012 --> 00:10:41.140
rug just to emphasize this
filming zone, by the way,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.850
I'm just drawing this furniture

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.395
in as placeholders,
but I am going to go through the process

00:10:46.395 --> 00:10:50.149
of selecting the furniture later,
but probably I'm going to go with IKEA

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:53.944
shelving anyways
because it's the most cost effective

00:10:54.028 --> 00:10:57.073
and I don't want to spend
a ton of money on the space.

00:10:57.615 --> 00:11:00.868
I'm going to try
flipping the TV and the couch

00:11:00.868 --> 00:11:05.206
so that when you walk
in, you see the couch instead of the TV.

00:11:05.289 --> 00:11:09.418
I kind of like this arrangement
because it creates more of an enclosure

00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:11.420
and I might end up changing it like this.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.799
So I kind of go back and forth
between these different elevations

00:11:14.799 --> 00:11:34.777
just to make sure that the space
feels coherent as a whole.

00:11:34.777 --> 00:11:38.614
Once I've got this design to the level
I want, I model this

00:11:38.614 --> 00:11:41.951
and to sketch up
with some generic furniture blocks

00:11:42.159 --> 00:11:44.495
just to get
a more accurate sense of scale.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:48.040
So as you can see,
I've created a little bit

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.753
more order into the space
by creating some continue lines

00:11:52.753 --> 00:11:57.007
with these shelves and by reinforcing
symmetry with the artwork.

00:11:57.007 --> 00:12:02.221
I'm also trying to make the space
feel a little bit more open by using low

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:07.768
furniture elements and a scones around
eye level to bring down the sight line.

00:12:07.935 --> 00:12:10.896
And I've added some tall artworks

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:14.567
and tall curtains to elongate the space
a little bit.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:19.655
I'm also creating two zones
with the shelving and the area rug.

00:12:19.947 --> 00:12:22.783
In the next phase,
I'm going to go through the process

00:12:22.783 --> 00:12:26.120
of developing a mood board
and a material palette.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:29.498
And at that point,
I'm also going to print out

00:12:29.498 --> 00:12:33.836
the perspectives from the SketchUp model
to play around with the materials

00:12:33.836 --> 00:12:37.840
so that I know exactly what pieces
I should buy before going to the store.

00:12:37.882 --> 00:12:41.719
If you're interested in this procreate
brush pack, I have everything from pens

00:12:41.719 --> 00:12:44.764
to markers
to brushes as well as to people's slides.

00:12:44.764 --> 00:12:46.515
I'm going to leave that
in the description.

00:12:46.515 --> 00:12:50.144
If you have any questions on anything that
I went through today, just let me know.

00:12:50.144 --> 00:12:51.395
Down at the comments.

00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:53.355
And if you like this video, please

00:12:53.355 --> 00:12:57.276
give me a thumbs up and stay tuned
for the rest of the renovation.

00:12:57.276 --> 00:13:00.404
It's going to be very exciting
and I will see you in the

00:13:00.404 --> 00:13:25.304
next video.

